    "product_id": "be9a05ad-11fb-47ae-95cc-09e72359e9d7",
    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KVEF 041056\nAFDVEF\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Las Vegas NV\n255 AM PST Wed Nov 4 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n* Temperatures will hover within a few degrees of seasonal normals \n  through the work week with otherwise quiet conditions.\n\n* Northerly winds will pick up Friday and Saturday, with stronger\n  speeds and elevated wave heights along the Colorado River\n  Valley.\n\n&&\n\n\n.DISCUSSION...Today through Tuesday.\n\nExpect dry conditions, temperatures within a few degrees of seasonal \nnormals, and afternoon breeziness through the work week. Shortwave \nridging will persist across the Desert Southwest for another day \nbefore a weak closed low pushes into the central California Coast \nand across the Great Basin on Wednesday. Impacts from this system \nwill be minimal, with only a slight uptick in wind speeds across the \nforecast area (10 to 20 mph gusts in the afternoon, with gusts to\n25 mph in the higher terrain). \n\nHeading into the weekend, heights will increase west-to-east thanks \nto a high pressure system in the eastern Pacific Ocean. This will \nallow temperatures to increase to slightly-above normal. In addition \nto this ridge, a shortwave will move along the upper-level flow and \nwill ultimately cut off in the southeastern Pacific Ocean late-week \ninto the weekend. Associated with this trough axis, an open wave \nwill push southeastward through our forecast area on Saturday. The \nresultant tightened pressure gradients across the region will \nincrease northerly wind gusts on Friday and Saturday to 15 to 25 \nmph. Along the Colorado River Valley, these north winds will be \nenhanced by local topographic features, which will allow speeds to \nrange 30 to 40 mph at times, with wave heights on Lake Mohave and \nLake Havasu to 3 feet. Will continue to assess the need for a wind-\nrelated headline as we get closer. Quiet conditions return for the\nremainder of the weekend into the start of next week.\n&&\n\n\n.AVIATION...For Harry Reid...For the 12Z Forecast \nPackage...Light winds will persist through the morning. Winds will \nbecome north around 18Z before veering northeast at 040-050 degrees \n(true) after 21Z, but speeds will likely remain under 8KT through \nthe afternoon at KLAS. KHND will also see winds under 8KT, but \nexpect northwest winds at KVGT around 10KT this afternoon with \nisolated gusts up to 20KT. Winds will shift to a typical overnight \nwind direction under 8KT around sunset. Moderate confidence that \nwinds will become northwest again at KLAS around 09Z then persist \ninto early Thursday morning when they will become light and variable \nagain. VFR skies expected through the period. \n\nFor the rest of southern Nevada, northwest Arizona and southeast \nCalifornia...For the 12Z Forecast Package...In general, light winds \nfavoring the north to northwest are expected this morning into the \nafternoon. The Owens Valley in California as well as the Colorado \nRiver Valley will see stronger north winds at 10-15KT, gusting over \n20KT at times this afternoon. Winds will diminish after sunset and \nlight winds return tonight. Mostly clear VFR skies are expected \nthrough the period.  \n\n&&\n\n\n.SPOTTER INFORMATION STATEMENT...Spotters are encouraged to report\nany significant weather or impacts according to standard operating\nprocedures.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...Soulat\nAVIATION...Nickerson\n\nFor more forecast information...see us on our webpage:\nhttps://weather.gov/lasvegas or follow us on Facebook and Twitter\n",
